        {
            "block_label": "figure_title",
            "block_content": "RECOMMENDATION STANDARDS",
            "block_bbox": [
                186,
                1201,
                420,
                1220
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": null,
            "group_id": 1
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "tapered hole in the spindle is “in-line” with the axis of the spindle in the vertical plane. The only change required for the set up in this test from the previous one, is to locate the DIAL button at the top of the test bar at the vertical diameter. A reading is taken as the carriage is traversed. If the unilateral tolerance indicated in the recommended standards is not exceeded, then the axis of the tapered hole is “in-line” with the axis of the spindle in the vertical plane.",
            "block_bbox": [
                93,
                57,
                505,
                324
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 2
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "In case the two tests show that the tapered hole is inaccurate and exceeds the limits allowed, the spindle must be removed from the tailstock. It must then be replaced with a new and accurate spindle. In lieu of this, the tapered hole of the defective spindle can be reworked. However, since this matter pertains to machine shop practice it will not be further discussed.",
            "block_bbox": [
                97,
                323,
                510,
                524
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 3
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "To reassure the critical reader, it will be emphasized that, finding a tailstock spindle with a defective tapered hole will not affect the alignment of the previously scraped bearing surfaces of the tailstock. Replacement of the spindle or reboring the taper hole will, however, be called for.",
            "block_bbox": [
                99,
                523,
                508,
                682
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 4
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Sec. 26.57",
            "block_bbox": [
                103,
                702,
                207,
                725
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 5
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The Head Stock of the Lathe",
            "block_bbox": [
                103,
                723,
                371,
                748
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 6
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The fixed member of the lathe which carries the work spindle supporting the live center, and the necessary mechanism to drive the work, is called the head stock. It is a solid one piece casting, as shown in Fig. 26.54. The member has two bearing surfaces which are scraped, namely:",
            "block_bbox": [
                104,
                767,
                512,
                927
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 7
        },
        {
            "block_label": "image",
            "block_content": "",
            "block_bbox": [
                113,
                976,
                504,
                1125
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": null,
            "group_id": 8
        },
        {
            "block_label": "figure_title",
            "block_content": "Fig. 26.53 Alignment of taper no. of tarsock in vertical plane.",
            "block_bbox": [
                118,
